# 🩺 CarePoint — Doctor Appointment Booking Platform

A full-stack telemedicine appointment booking platform built with the MERN stack. Patients can search for doctors, book appointments based on real-time availability, pay online, chat with their doctor, and access prescriptions — all in one place. Doctors manage their schedules and consultations, while admins verify doctors and oversee the platform.

---

## 📌 Overview

CarePoint connects **Patients**, **Doctors**, and **Admins** through a role-based platform:

- **Patients** search doctors by specialization, book available time slots, pay securely, chat with their doctor, and keep a running medical history.
- **Doctors** set their weekly availability, manage incoming appointments, consult via chat/video, and issue digital prescriptions.
- **Admins** verify doctor credentials before they go live, moderate users, resolve disputes, and monitor platform analytics.

This project was built to demonstrate real-world full-stack engineering: role-based access control, race-condition-safe booking logic, state-machine-driven workflows, real-time communication, and payment processing — not just CRUD operations.

---

## ✨ Key Features

- 🔐 **Role-based authentication** (Patient / Doctor / Admin) with JWT
- ✅ **Doctor verification workflow** — doctors can't go live until admin-approved
- 📅 **Dynamic slot generation** — availability is computed on-demand from recurring weekly rules + exceptions (leave days), not pre-stored in the database
- 🔒 **Race-condition-safe booking** — a database-level partial unique index prevents two patients from ever double-booking the same slot
- 🔁 **Appointment state machine** — `pending → confirmed → completed / cancelled / no_show`
- 💳 **Stripe payment integration** with refund handling on valid cancellations
- 💬 **Real-time chat** between patient and doctor (Socket.io)
- 📹 **Video consultation** link generation
- 📄 **Digital prescriptions** tied to each appointment, visible in patient medical history
- ⭐ **Ratings & reviews** with denormalized aggregate scores on doctor profiles
- 🔔 **Notifications** (in-app + email) including automated appointment reminders via cron jobs
- 📊 **Admin analytics dashboard** — revenue, appointment volume, top specializations, doctor performance

---

## 🛠️ Tech Stack

| Layer | Technology |
|---|---|
| Frontend | React (Vite), Redux Toolkit / Zustand, Tailwind CSS |
| Backend | Node.js, Express.js |
| Database | MongoDB, Mongoose |
| Real-time | Socket.io |
| Payments | Stripe |
| File Storage | Cloudinary |
| Auth | JWT, bcrypt |
| Scheduled Jobs | node-cron |
| Deployment | Vercel (frontend/admin), Render/Railway (backend), MongoDB Atlas |

---

## 🏗️ Architecture

The project is split into three independent applications:

```
doctor-appointment-platform/
├── frontend/   # Unified Patient + Doctor web app (role-based rendering)
├── admin/      # Separate admin panel (verification queue, analytics)
└── backend/    # Single Express API serving all three roles
```

**Why separate the admin panel?** Isolating admin as its own app improves security (admin bundle/routes never ship to regular users) and keeps the main app lightweight, since admin-only libraries (charts, data tables) don't bloat the patient/doctor bundle.

**Why unify Patient and Doctor into one frontend?** They share the majority of the UI — auth, chat, profile, order/appointment tracking — so splitting them would mean duplicating most of the app. Role-based conditional rendering (`ProtectedRoute` + `user.role` checks) keeps the codebase DRY.

### Backend structure

```
backend/
├── config/        # DB, Stripe, Cloudinary, Nodemailer configs
├── models/        # Mongoose schemas
├── controllers/   # Thin HTTP handlers
├── services/      # Business logic (slotService, appointmentService, paymentService)
├── middleware/     # auth, roleCheck, errorHandler, validateRequest
├── routes/
├── sockets/        # Real-time chat event handlers
├── jobs/           # Cron jobs (appointment reminders)
└── utils/
```

---

## 🧩 Design Patterns Used

| Pattern | Where it's applied |
|---|---|
| **MVC** | Overall backend structure |
| **Service Layer** | Business logic (e.g. `slotService`, `appointmentService`) kept separate from route controllers |
| **State Pattern** | Appointment status transitions are explicitly validated, not free-form |
| **Strategy Pattern** | Notification delivery method (email vs in-app) selected at runtime |
| **Observer Pattern** | Socket.io events; rating recalculation triggered on new review |
| **Factory Pattern** | Notification payload construction based on event type |
| **Chain of Responsibility** | Express middleware stack: `auth → roleCheck → doctorApprovalCheck → validateRequest → controller` |
| **Singleton** | Database connection, Stripe client instance |
| **Optimistic Concurrency Control** | Partial unique compound index on `Appointment` (doctor + date + startTime) prevents double-booking at the database level, without needing application-side locks |

---

## 🗄️ Database Schema

Core collections: `User`, `DoctorProfile`, `Availability`, `Appointment`, `Prescription`, `Conversation`, `Message`, `Review`, `Notification`, `Transaction`.

```
User (patient) ──┐
                  ├──> Appointment ──> DoctorProfile ──> User (doctor)
User (doctor)─────┘        │                │
                            │                └──> Availability
                            ├──> Conversation ──> Message[]
                            ├──> Prescription
                            ├──> Review
                            └──> Transaction[]
```

Notable design decisions:
- **Availability is stored as recurring rules + exceptions**, not pre-generated time slots — bookable slots are computed at request time, keeping the database lean and always in sync with the doctor's latest schedule.
- **`Appointment` has a partial unique index** on `{ doctor, date, startTime }` scoped to active statuses, so double-booking is rejected at the database layer even under concurrent requests.

